I redid my blog. By "redid" I mean "started using a different static site generator." My
previous layout looked like this:
[Something ironic about claiming personality in a carbon copy of other sites]($image.asset('old-look.png').size(800,0))

I originally built this site using [hugo](https://gohugo.io/), probably the most popular
static site generator out there (at least for stuff hosted on GitHub pages) and the
[congo](https://jpanther.github.io/congo/) theme for it, one of the more popular options.
To be clear, both of these things are excellent and gave me no grief at all. Hugo works
well and is easy to use, and congo definitely looks great, with what I would call a very
"clean" layout.

So why am I now building the site with [zine](https://zine-ssg.io/), a significantly less
"batteries-included" option that's only been around for a year and change? Why mess with a
good thing?

The short answer is that my website didn't really feel like it was "mine." The long answer
is the remaining [insert-word-count] words below.

[]($section)
## What Even is "Mine?"
Oh fuck yeah we're jumping into the existentialism right off the rip. How dare you
think any different.

This is well-tread ground. I'm not a philosopher, and don't claim to be. I'm just a guy
that writes a lot of mediocre software and runs a lot, and think those two things give him
a unique personality. Individualism, the human desire to create and to share, all of those
things have a myriad of discussion written by people way smarter than I am.

With all that in mind, something about my previous website just felt... off. It felt like
it was almost performative: what started as truly a way for me to throw my writing out
into the world changed into somewhere where I felt that I had to present myself in a
specific way. Add to that the weird dichotomy of wanting people to read my writing, but
not wanting to share it widely for fear of coming off as narcissistic (whether that's
feeling holds merit or not is outside the context of this discussion), and I ended up in a
place that just didn't feel "mine."

I think it really started to itch at me when I was browsing Hacker News one morning and
came across two separate articles on two different websites using the same congo template,
and it left a bad taste in my mouth. It felt like I was trying to be unique and creative
only as much as was convenient for me (writing), and not in how I presented myself outside
of it.

[]($section)
## Hackable Heart
When I really dug down into why I was feeling so *off* about how things were, it came down
to a pretty simple fact: I really fucking like to tinker with things, especially software.
Like, a problematic amount. I don't claim to be a greybeard hacker who runs his own
home-rolled kernel, but I, like many others, just like fucking around with the tools I use
(and often breaking them).

How does that relate to my website, which is built (both past and present) with
open-source tools? Well, the short answer is that when I was using hugo/congo, I didn't
really have a lot of control over the actual layout of my site as a whole - I just wrote
some markdown files and then the templating engine layed it all out. Sure, I could fork
the project and change it however I wanted, and there's stuff like hugo templates (which I
did actually do a bit of customization with), but it felt like there were just so many
layers between me and the actual HTML/CSS that beams into your browser.

This is where zine really shines. [SuperHTML](https://zine-ssg.io/docs/superhtml/) and
[SuperMD](https://zine-ssg.io/docs/supermd/) are really brilliant ways to give me direct
control over the structure of my content while also introducting just enough logic to do
some fancy stuff. For example, I like pinning a few posts on my homepage, ones that I
think are good introductions to my writing style or that I'm particularly proud of. In
hugo/congo, this was a two hour long struggle of playing with article weights, trying to
add an overlay template, forgetting multiple closing curly braces, and then finally
getting it to mostly work. In zine, it took me approximately 3 minutes having never
touched the system before: add a custom `pinned` frontmatter property to any article I
want, and then filter for it in my HTML source to display the list. Simple, intuitive and
way more powerful in what I can do with it.

[]($section)
## An Eternal WIP
Anyway, the site is and will continue to be an ever-evolving work in progress. The code
syntax highlighting, though it now uses a color scheme I like, is still super half-assed.
The quote blocks look OK, but not perfect. I'm still undecided on the background color,
and the navigation scheme is largely the same as the default zine output, with a couple
small tweaks. I don't claim to be a designer either, I just slap code into my editor and
hope it makes something that looks reasonably decent. But, the good part is, now I know
the site inside and out. And, with how new zine is (and how much more I enjoy zig than
go), I'm super excited to actually contribute back to the ecosystem.

Things aren't done on the hosting side either. Currently, this site is just tossed onto
GitHub pages for simplicity, but I'd really love to get it running on my ~old laptop
shoved into the corner~ home server, which currently runs a bunch of useful stuff, but
only inside my network. That change will have to come in the future, though. For now, I'm
just glad that what I put onto the internet feels more authentically like me.
